{"author":{"id":"f943aa1c5ee078b881a8e97535a4e2ccb401d01ca00f4128126befa9cca58a32"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792271651,"nonce":"BxBv4a9EtmZ/akQG7jwrOV3uN2M=","title":"Fix the 2026-10-17 review: 15 bugs in git-board and the git-bug fork","message":"A deep review of everything shipped on 2026-10-17 found 13 correctness bugs and 2 slow paths. Scope: the fork's `bridge-sync` branch `ca5eff0a..906d1bcf` (one-issue sync, `#n` in titles, link/unlink/take) and git-board `02d6efb~1..c3be93b` (Settings tabs, Add a forge, branches, Sync page, query, numbers M1, roles M2, inbox). Nothing is fixed yet. `numbers-ui` and `prd/gate`, merged afterwards (main 2b26234), were not in scope.\n\nThe top three (1, 2, 8) were checked by hand against the code. **Fix first: 1, 3, 7 and 8.** They create duplicate forge issues, write wrong references to co2's issues, erase forge labels, and give two issues the same CO2 number.\n\n\nPaste this into a fresh Claude Code session started in `~/works/git/github/git-bug`:\n\n\u003e Fix the findings of the 2026-10-17 review, listed in git-board's issue \"Fix the 2026-10-17 review: 15 bugs in git-board and the git-bug fork\" (`git -C ~/works/git/github/git-board bug ls` to find it, then `bug show \u003cid\u003e`). Read the memory files first (bridge-sync-effort, git-board-ways-of-working).\n\u003e Work in two worktrees in parallel, one agent each:\n\u003e - fork findings 1–6 on branch `review-fixes` off `bridge-sync` (worktree `~/works/git/github/git-bug-review-fixes`);\n\u003e - git-board findings 7–15 on branch `fix/review-2026-10-17` off `main` (worktree `~/works/git/github/git-board-review-fixes`).\n\u003e\n\u003e For each finding: re-read the code (line numbers may have moved), write a failing test that shows the failure scenario, then fix it at its root, one commit per finding. If a finding turns out wrong, say why in the issue instead of fixing it. Comment on the issue as each one lands.\n\u003e Checks before reporting back:\n\u003e - fork: `go test ./...`, gofmt from Go 1.26 (`$(GOTOOLCHAIN=go1.26.0 go env GOROOT)/bin/gofmt -l .`), and the generated docs.\n\u003e - git-board: `make ci`, `npm test` and `npm run typecheck` in `web/`, and gofmt from Go 1.26.\n\u003e\n\u003e Rules:\n\u003e - Never push, sync or open a PR on co2-calculator.\n\u003e - Live tests only on scratch repos (guilbep/git-bug-sync-test, guilbep/git-board-test, gitlab.epfl.ch 42400) or local bare remotes.\n\u003e - Never run `git bug bridge auth`.\n\u003e - Push nothing and tag nothing until I confirm in this session. Then: merge `review-fixes` into `bridge-sync`, tag v0.11.1-sync.4 (pre-release, proper notes), bump git-board's go.mod pin, merge, push.\n\u003e\n\u003e Coordinate with the gate session (M4: PR review comments in the issue). It builds on `review-sync` off `bridge-sync` and touches the same functions in `bridge/core/syncpush.go` (pushChanges, planPush, the comment-edit loop). Agree on who rebases.\n\n\n- [ ] **1. One-issue sync can create a duplicate forge issue.** `bridge/core/syncpull.go:57`\n  - **Failure:** `PullIssue` answers \"not on this forge yet\" without the marker search that a full Pull does (findBug → MarkerID). The issue can exist without this clone knowing: another clone synced it, or a push crashed between CreateIssue and Commit. `PushBug` then creates a second forge issue. git-board's per-issue Sync now goes through this path too.\n  - **Fix:** before creating, look for this bug's git-bug marker on the forge, as Pull does. A forge search for the marker is fine if listing every issue costs too much.\n- [ ] **2. Gitea's Fetch accepts pull requests.** `bridge/gitea/remote.go:198`\n  - **Failure:** `Issues()` skips PRs (`gi.PullRequest != nil`), `Fetch` doesn't. So `git bug bridge link \u003cbug\u003e gitea 12` (or a `/pulls/12` URL) links a bug to PR #12, and sync then writes comments, title, state and labels onto the PR. GitHub and GitLab already refuse this.\n  - **Fix:** refuse in Fetch when `gi.PullRequest != nil`.\n- [ ] **3. References to issues from the old bridges are translated wrongly.** `bridge/core/syncpush.go:603-605`\n  - **Failure:** `newRefIndex` puts every legacy bug (`github-id`/`gitlab-id`) under one key per kind, `legacy:github` or `legacy:gitlab`, whatever its project. Two things break:\n    - Numbers from two projects collide: `#5` can become another project's issue.\n    - A legacy bug is never indexed under its own project's remote key. On co2, bug 228 is GitHub #2518 (legacy) and GitLab #77. A GitLab `see #77` sent to GitHub becomes a GitLab link, not `#2518`.\n  - **Fix:** index each legacy bug under the remote key of the project in its `\u003ckind\u003e-url`.\n  - **Note:** this touches co2 directly, since all its issues are legacy.\n- [ ] **4. One broken issue makes every one-issue sync report an error.** `bridge/core/syncpush.go:58`\n  - **Failure:** `PushBug` builds its ref index with `e.excerpts(out)`, which sends an ExportError for any other bug whose excerpt doesn't resolve. git-board's dry-run gate then refuses the real run.\n  - **Fix:** while building the index, skip excerpts that don't resolve without reporting them, or report only for the bug being synced.\n- [ ] **5. Link resends titles that contain `#refs`.** `bridge/core/synclink.go:175`\n  - **Failure:** titles count as in sync only when the forge title equals `snap.Title`, not the title rendered for that forge (`e.title(snap, refs)`). The next sync sends an unnecessary SetTitle, and `--dry-run` says \"would send: title\".\n  - **Fix:** compare with the rendered title.\n- [ ] **6. `bridge take` says it took something when it took nothing.** `commands/bridge/bridge_take.go:52`\n  - **Failure:** every printed result counts as a change, warnings included. On an issue deleted on the forge: no \"nothing to take\" line, and it exits 0.\n  - **Fix:** count only results that changed something.\n\n\n- [ ] **7. Send erases labels added on the forge.** `internal/gitbugio/identities.go:216`\n  - **Failure:** Send pushes without pulling first, and the engine's pushChanges replaces the forge's whole label set (SetLabels). A label someone added on GitHub since the last Get or Sync is removed there. Comment texts edited on both sides are overwritten with no conflict. The UI warning only mentions titles and states.\n  - **Fix:** do a Get first, or make Send refuse while the forge has unpulled changes. A better long-term fix, in the fork, is for the engine to send label changes (added/removed since the base) instead of the full set.\n- [ ] **8. Two issues can get the same project number.** `internal/gitbugio/numbers.go:117`\n  - **Failure:** `Counter.NumberOf` takes a GitHub number below Start from any forge, including one marked Gone (unlinked or deleted). BareNumber, HasNumber and HasForgeNumber all skip gone forges. Unlink A from #2518 and link #2518 to B: both show CO2-2518, and A never gets its own number.\n  - **Fix:** skip forges with `Gone != \"\"`. A gone number then needs a line of its own on the next numbering.\n- [ ] **9. Unlinked issues from the old bridges still look linked on the board.** `internal/gitbugio/thread.go:305`\n  - **Failure:** `forgesOf` reads Gone only next to `sync:\u003ckey\u003e:url` keys. A legacy bug has only `github-url`, so after Unlink (which writes `sync:\u003ckey\u003e:gone`) its forge stays live in ListIssues. Filters, branch names and the TUI still answer `#2518`. Only the panel (forgeBridges) shows it gone.\n  - **Fix:** apply `sync:\u003ckey\u003e:gone` to the legacy forge with the same URL.\n- [ ] **10. The Gone mark can land on the wrong forge.** `internal/gitbugio/thread.go:297`\n  - **Failure:** Gone is set on `forges[len(forges)-1]`, but `add()` skips a URL it already has. With a repeated URL, a live forge gets marked gone.\n  - **Fix:** have `add()` return the index it used.\n- [ ] **11. Other tabs don't see new numbers after Push on origin.** `cmd/git-board-server/events.go:134`\n  - **Failure:** `POST /api/remotes/origin/push` now numbers issues, but its event is classed `sync`, not `issues`. Other tabs keep showing the issues without numbers.\n  - **Fix:** class that push as `issues` too.\n- [ ] **12. `lint --create-labels` fails when the issue-template check can't run.** `cmd/git-board/lint.go:135`\n  - **Failure:** `gh repo view` fails (no default repo, non-GitHub origin), or a template has no front matter: \"one or more repos failed\", non-zero exit, although every label exists. This breaks CI use.\n  - **Fix:** report the template check as a warning.\n- [ ] **13. The board crashes when no issue matches a filter.** `/api/facets`, `cmd/git-board-server/server.go:295` → `board.Facets`\n  - **Failure:** the answer is `null` instead of `[]`, and the board throws \"Cannot read properties of null (reading 'map')\". Found while testing Settings › Numbers.\n  - **Fix:** return an empty list from `board.Facets`.\n\n\n- [ ] **14. The inbox repeats work for every recent issue.** `cmd/git-board-server/inbox.go:122`\n  - **Failure:** each request calls Thread for every issue updated in 14 days. That re-reads refs/board/seq, SyncBridges and the config each time, and runs FollowedViews twice. It happens on every `issues` and `inbox` event, in every tab.\n  - **Fix:** read them once per request.\n- [ ] **15. Every `GET /api/remotes` walks whole histories.** `internal/gitbugio/remotes.go:119`\n  - **Failure:** `ahead()` → `has()` lists the whole history of each diverged bug or identity on every call. InboxPage calls it twice when it opens, ForgeSyncPage on every event.\n  - **Fix:** one ancestry check per ref pair, or a cache by hash pair.\n\n\n- `ForgeSyncPage.vue:255` has its own `forgeLabel` and name map. Use the one in `issuesync.ts`.\n- git-board's `GetForges`/`SendForges` rebuild one-way runs (lock, last sync time) that could be a mode of the fork's sync run.","files":null}]}